Double Lower Limb Kinematics-based Invariant Extended Kalman Filtering for Pedestrian Inertial State Estimation *
Fei Guo National Key Laboratory of Human Factors Engineering China Astronaut Research and Training Center Beijing, China guofei_bit@126.com
Abstract—This paper combined the double lower limbs and waist as rigid bodies. Each angle of hip, knee, and ankle joint can be measured through several Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU), formulating the double lower kinematics. Focusing on the model uncertainty of stance and swing phase switching within gait cycle, a novel invariant extended Kalman filter (EKF) was contrived to produce state estimation. Towards the rotation path, the people executed walking locomotion in simulation. Compared to the classical EKF, the more rapid error convergence for pedestrian state estimation is accomplished using the invariant extended Kalman filter (InEKF). This methodology demonstrated the validity, feasibility and accuracy of pedestrian inertial state estimation with InEKF, which expanded the application scope of inertial measurement apparatus.
Keywords—Invariant Kalman filter, lower limb kinematics, state estimation, Inertial Measurement Unit
I. INTRODUCTION
With the continuous increase in people’s demand for precise
position and motion state, the widespread employ of portable
device promotes the development of localization system. The
technology of motion state acquirement has applied in several
aspects of life, including the human-machine interaction[1],
motion capture[2], and exoskeleton[3]. The IMU-based
pedestrian state estimation is a kind of micro-electromechanical
system, whose benefits are high autonomy, intellectuality,
reliability and low cost.
On account of the existence of stance phase that foot can
maintain relative stillstand with the ground. The Zero Velocity
Update (ZUPT) solved the error accumulation issues[4]. During
the Zero-Velocity period, Kalman Filer is usually used to correct
systematic errors. Specifically, importing the observed quantity
is to construct constrain and correct error[5][6]. To restrain the
error accumulation resulted from integral operator, the
Pedestrian Dead Reckoning[7] computes the real-time position
of pedestrian through monitoring the locomotion data containing
the step length, step frequency, and orientation. The common
approaches of step length works through fitting acceleration
including the linear and nonlinear model. However, the step size
estimation determines the parameters through collecting gait
features. So the accuracy of step length model relies on the gait
statistical information.
The discrete and continuous states are scheduled in
pedestrian locomotion. Apart from the stance phase, a typical
gait involves the swing phase which forms variable-speed
trajectory. The selection of sensor position can influence the
measured acceleration variation when foot contacts with ground,
leading to the low zero-speed detection accuracy[8]. Placing the
IMU on feet, the instantaneous impact generated from touching
the ground[9] causes extreme value in acceleration measurement.
The faster the walking speed is, the shorter the relative stationary
time of ground touching will be. It becomes apparent that the
error of inertial system grows bigger. Whereas, while putting
IMU on the lower leg and waist, the occurrence of sensor data
abnormity originated from strenuous exercise decreases due to
the lower impulse.
Adding sensors[10] can increase the observed value to boost
the positional accuracy of system. But utilizing the visual
sensors to assistant localization enhances system cost[11]. Even
though novel sensor arrangement can boost the localization
accuracy, but also it may reduce the system portability and
increase complexity.
These days the disparate Kalman filters[12] have been used
to estimate body pose and velocity through the nonlinear
forward kinematics[13]. The EKF is also applied to additionally
process the inaccuracy of the nonlinear kinematics chain and
sensor placement imperfections. Nevertheless, the EKF has
pretty high requirements[14] for the accuracy of initial values. If
the difference between initial value setup of state and practical
situation emerges pretty big, the filter is hard to converge.
Furthermore, the EKF can leads to inconsistency issues[15]. The
EKF calculate current covariance matrix through linearization of
previous state, while the actual value of current state covariance
matrix does not stay the same with it. The InEKF applies group
transformation to state variables based on Lie algebra and
changes the defined way of errors. The independence of the
system matrix and the state estimated values are realized,
expanding the state space that ensures the convergency of state
and covariance[16].
This work used the pedestrian waist and double lower limbs,
which are considered as rigid bodies under the condition without
bringing new type sensor. Different with step length estimation
based on empirically fitted model such as pedestrian dead
reckoning (PDR)[17] and zero velocity update(ZUPT)[18], this
work placed IMU on waist, thighs and calves of two legs, as well
as feet. Fashion the low-limbs kinematics to estimate the
pedestrian motion state. Fusing IMU data with InEKF restrained
estimation error, furtherly improving the accuracy.
